      'Each round can be compared with the previous ones because the framework stays stable. Scores are read on a scale with mean 500 and standard deviation 100, not as a percentage.',
    whatS3: 'Built by the countries',
    whatS3d:
      'Items are developed by expert teams from the participating countries themselves, not by the OECD alone. That is what makes results comparable across different systems.',

    frameworksTitle: 'The three domain frameworks',
    frameworksNote:
      'Each domain assesses different processes. Knowing them avoids the most common mistake: practising only the maths one.',

    mathTitle: 'Mathematics',
    mathProcesses: 'Processes',
    mathProcess1: 'Formulate',
    mathProcess1d:
      'Translate a real-world problem into mathematical language: spot which mathematics is in it and give it structure.',
    mathProcess2: 'Employ',
    mathProcess2d:
      'Apply concepts, facts, procedures and reasoning to a problem that has already been formulated, to reach a conclusion.',
    mathProcess3: 'Interpret and evaluate',
    mathProcess3d:
      'Come back to the original context: check whether the result is reasonable and makes sense in the real world.',
    mathContent: 'Content',
    mathContentValue:
      'Quantity · Uncertainty · Change and relationships · Patterns. Two items usually sit on mathematical modelling, the heart of reasoning.',
    mathContexts: 'Contexts',
    mathContextsValue: 'Personal · Social · Global · Academic',
    mathContextsNote:
      'Most released items use the personal context: everyday money, transport, unplanned household tasks.',

    readingTitle: 'Reading',
    readingBody:
      'The reading framework organises comprehension into three broad strands: locate information, comprehend and infer, and reflect and evaluate. Each text asks for something different, and difficulty grows when a student has to hold an inference across more than one sentence.',
    readingNote:
      'The PISA 2018 released items we use as anchors are CR551, CR548, CR557 and CR571. Notice the style: questions that send you back to the text more than once.',

    scienceTitle: 'Science',
    scienceBody:
      'Science is assessed with three competencies: explaining phenomena scientifically, interpreting and evaluating data and evidence, and producing or evaluating scientific knowledge. The core competency is reasoning with evidence, not memorising facts.',
